# QudoSSL Commercial
#
# This repository vendors upstream OpenSSL 3.5.7 unmodified. The tree is built
# in place, so OpenSSL writes build products into its own source directories.
# Those products are architecture-specific and must never be committed: a
# darwin64-arm64 object file in the repository would be built into a Linux
# container, and generated assembly would be compiled for the wrong platform.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. In .gitignore the LAST matching pattern wins, so the generic
# artefact patterns (*.o, *.a, ...) are at the bottom. Putting them higher lets
# a later re-inclusion such as `!openssl/fuzz/*.*` silently drag ~400 object
# files back in, which is exactly what happened while writing this file.
#
# If you are unsure whether a file belongs, the test is whether a clean
# checkout plus `make -C build all` reproduces it.

# --- Configure output -------------------------------------------------------
# configdata.pm and Makefile encode the host target. A committed copy makes
# every other machine build for whatever platform configured it last.
openssl/configdata.pm
openssl/Makefile
openssl/*.pc
openssl/providers/*.pc
openssl/apps/openssl
openssl/apps/CA.pl
openssl/apps/tsget.pl
openssl/tools/c_rehash

# --- generated headers and sources ------------------------------------------
# crypto/buildinf.h is the dangerous one: it contains a literal
#   #define PLATFORM "platform: darwin64-arm64"
# and make will not regenerate a copy that already looks current, so a
# committed one would make a Linux container report macOS.
openssl/crypto/buildinf.h
openssl/include/openssl/configuration.h
openssl/include/openssl/opensslv.h
openssl/apps/progs.h
openssl/apps/progs.c
openssl/test/buildtest_c_*.c

# --- generated assembly -----------------------------------------------------
# OpenSSL generates .S from the perl scripts in <dir>/asm/*.pl, writing the
# output one level up. The generated flavour is host-specific — macOS output
# omits the `.arch armv8-a+crypto` directives GNU as requires, so building it
# on Linux fails with "selected processor does not support `aesimc'".
#
# The .S files INSIDE an asm/ directory are different: genuine checked-in
# upstream sources for platforms with no perl generator (ia64, s390x, sparc).
# They are re-included below and must stay — dropping upstream source from a
# product whose whole proposition is "unmodified upstream" would defeat it.
openssl/crypto/*.S
openssl/crypto/*.s
openssl/crypto/**/*.S
openssl/crypto/**/*.s
!openssl/crypto/**/asm/*.S
!openssl/crypto/**/asm/*.s
!openssl/crypto/sparccpuid.S
!openssl/crypto/ia64cpuid.S

# --- compiled test and fuzz binaries ----------------------------------------
# 344 of these exist after a build. They are extensionless, so no extension
# pattern catches them, and several are over 5 MB.
#
# Verified rather than assumed: every extensionless file directly under these
# two directories is a compiled binary, and every source beside them carries an
# extension. So exclude the directory contents, then re-include anything with
# an extension and any subdirectory.
openssl/test/*
!openssl/test/*.*
!openssl/test/*/
openssl/fuzz/*
!openssl/fuzz/*.*
!openssl/fuzz/*/

# --- test and run-time output -----------------------------------------------
openssl/test/test-runs/
openssl/test/*.log
openssl/fuzz/corpora/
build/out/

# --- local state and secrets ------------------------------------------------
# fipsmodule.cnf carries an integrity HMAC computed for one specific fips
# module on one specific machine. It is generated by `openssl fipsinstall`.
openssl/ssl/fipsmodule.cnf
*.pem.local
*.key.local

# --- compiled artefacts — LAST, so nothing above can re-include them --------
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.d
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.map

# --- editor / OS noise ------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# --- not product source -----------------------------------------------------
# Meeting notes / working docs, not repo source.
*.docx
# Local pre-submodule backup of the 3.5.4 tree.
/openssl-fips.local.bak/

# --- source distribution output ---------------------------------------------
/dist/
